# @LOOM_PROJECT_NAME@

This application was generated by [loom](https://github.com/Arcadyi/loom) with the
[loom](https://github.com/Arcadyi/loom) styling library pre-wired.

```sh
loom doctor
loom dev
```

Edit `qml/Main.qml` while the application is running. QML, JavaScript, and asset
changes reload in the existing process. C++ and build-system changes trigger an
incremental rebuild and restart.

## Project map

```text
qml/Main.qml                  application window and reload state
qml/pages/HomePage.qml        first screen and responsive layout
qml/components/FeatureCard.qml reusable starter component
design/tokens.json            palette, themes, recipes, and lint policy
src/main.cpp                  native application entry point
tests/tst_smoke.cpp           fast CTest smoke target
```

The generated screen is deliberately made from ordinary project components,
not a framework-owned demo. Replace `HomePage`, copy `FeatureCard`, or strip it
back as the product takes shape.

```sh
loom add page Settings      # qml/pages/Settings.qml
loom add component Badge    # qml/components/Badge.qml
```

Nothing has to be registered: the QML root is globbed, so a new file is
compiled in on the next configure.

## CLion

Open the project directory and let the initial CMake reload finish. The scaffold
enables CLion's QML language server and language-server completion for the
generated CMake profiles, and selects the Loom `qmlls` proxy from the
installation that created this project. Normal Qt/QML completion and Loom
completion inside `Lo.style` strings therefore use the same server.

The generated `.idea` directory is machine-local and ignored by Git. If Loom or
Qt moves, use **Settings | Languages & Frameworks | QML** to update the Qt tools
directory, or regenerate the project-local settings with a current `loom new`.

## Styling with loom

The starter QML uses both of loom's layers:

```qml
import Loom

// Typed design tokens.
Label { color: Loom.color.foreground }

// Utility strings, with state and responsive variants.
Rectangle { Lo.style: "bg-surface rounded-lg hover:bg-surface-alt transition-colors" }
```

`Loom.theme = "dark"` switches every semantic token at runtime; this project
already persists the theme across hot reloads via `loomSaveState`.

## Finding loom

The generated `CMakeLists.txt` first uses the Loom CMake package belonging to
the CLI that created this project. This lets CLion and other IDEs configure the
project even when they did not inherit the terminal's `PATH`. If the project is
copied to another machine, that hint is ignored once the original installation
does not exist and `find_package(loom CONFIG REQUIRED)` uses CMake's usual
search paths.

If loom is installed somewhere else — including straight out of its build tree
— put that path on `CMAKE_PREFIX_PATH` before configuring or running a loom
command:

```sh
export CMAKE_PREFIX_PATH=/path/to/loom/build   # or an install prefix
loom build
```

CMake reads that environment variable natively, and loom's child processes
inherit it, so `build`, `test`, `lint` and `dev` all pick it up.
